  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Denture-related traumatic ulcers (K12.1)</content>. Both ulcers healed within two weeks of removing the source of trauma, which confirms denture trauma; no biopsy is needed. [S]<list><item>Lower denture flange relieved and the denture relined with a tissue conditioner today.</item><item>Oral mucosal examination at every visit.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Complete edentulism, ACP diagnostic index class III (K08.103)</content>. Dr. Okoro-Lindqvist finds complete edentulism of both jaws with about 14 mm of mandibular bone on the panoramic radiograph, a flat lower ridge and a high muscle attachment: American College of Prosthodontists class III. Worn dentures with a reduced vertical dimension explain the pain, the instability and the change in his lips. A two-implant overdenture is the first-choice standard for the edentulous lower jaw; his health, normal hemoglobin A1c and lack of tobacco use make him a good candidate. [S]<list><item>New upper and lower complete dentures, the lower designed for conversion.</item><item>Cone beam CT of the mandible in two weeks.</item><item>Two lower implants after the new dentures are delivered, with stud attachments after about three months.</item><item>Daily reading aloud to adapt his speech to the new dentures.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Painful, loose dentures (K08.9)</content>. Explained by the diagnosis; this presenting problem is closed. [S]<list><item>Followed under complete edentulism.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Unintended weight loss with nutritional risk (R63.4)</content>. Weight stable to slightly up on a soft high-protein diet and a supplement; the weight-loss tests at intake were normal, so poor chewing is the likely cause. [S]<list><item>Continue the supplement twice a day.</item><item>Repeat the MNA-SF at about three months.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Oral mucosal examination and oral cancer screening (lips, cheeks, tongue, floor of mouth, palate, ridges, neck nodes); re-examination of a mucosal ulcer two weeks after removing the irritant</td><td>Every visit; ulcer re-examined at two weeks</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Both ulcers healed at two weeks; no red or white patch or mass; biopsy not needed</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Denture fit: retention, stability, occlusion, vertical dimension and sore spots</td><td>At diagnosis, 24 hours to one week after delivery, after each adjustment, then yearly</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Lower denture unstable with reduced vertical dimension; worn teeth; ACP class III; replacement planned</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Panoramic radiograph before prosthodontic treatment</td><td>At diagnosis</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No retained roots or bony lesion; mandibular height about 14 mm</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Cone beam CT of the implant sites</td><td>Before implant surgery</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Booked in two weeks</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Medical fitness for implants: hemoglobin A1c and review of bone and healing risks</td><td>Before implant surgery</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Hemoglobin A1c 5.4% at intake; no bisphosphonate, steroid, radiation or tobacco</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Weight</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>92.8 kg, up 0.4 kg from intake</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Nutrition screen (MNA-SF, recorded as narrative)</td><td>At baseline when at risk, after about three months, and at six months</td><td>Up to date</td><td>MNA-SF 10 of 14 at intake; repeat at about three months</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Chewing and speech (patient-rated chewing ability out of 10; clarity of speech in reading aloud)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Chewing 4 of 10; lower denture lifts in speech</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Analgesic use within the daily maximum</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Acetaminophen two or three times a week, well under 3 g a day</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Denture hygiene: daily cleaning, dentures out overnight, ridge and tongue cleaning</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Cleans both dentures daily; lower denture out between meals and overnight</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Prosthodontics consultation</td><td>At diagnosis</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen by Dr. Okoro-Lindqvist today</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Oral ulcers healed within two weeks of relieving the denture, without need for a biopsy. Outcome by 1790-02-02: Both ulcers healed at two weeks; no biopsy needed [S]</item><item>New complete dentures that are comfortable and stable for eating and speaking. In progress; target 1790-04-13 [S]</item><item>A two-implant lower overdenture in function. In progress; target 1790-07-13 [S]</item><item>Regain weight and a normal nutrition screen. In progress; target 1790-07-13 [S]</item><item>Speak in public without the lower denture moving. In progress; target 1790-07-13 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Classification system for complete edentulism. The American College of Prosthodontics (1999); Evidence-based guidelines for the care and maintenance of complete dentures: a publication of the American College of Prosthodontists (2011); The McGill consensus statement on overdentures. Mandibular two-implant overdentures as first choice standard of care for edentulous patients (2002); The York consensus statement on implant-supported overdentures (2009); Evidence-based clinical practice guideline for the evaluation of potentially malignant disorders in the oral cavity: A report of the American Dental Association (2017); Validation of the Mini Nutritional Assessment short-form (MNA-SF): a practical tool for identification of nutritional status (2009)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Cone beam CT in two weeks; new dentures over the next four weeks with prosthodontics; preventive visit in six weeks; twelve-week review. Call for a new mouth ulcer, swelling or bleeding. Next scheduled visit 1790-03-16: periodic adult preventive visit. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
